    <div class="jr-answer">
      <h2>The short answer</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>No supplement beats quadriceps strengthening and weight loss for knee pain, and
        the two most people are actually taking — glucosamine and chondroitin — are now strongly
        recommended against by the American College of Rheumatology.</strong> Of what remains,
        collagen peptides at 5–10g daily have the largest trial base, Boswellia serrata the
        strongest recent comparative result, and vitamin D3 matters only if you are deficient.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Best supported:</strong> collagen peptides (an 11-trial meta-analysis in 870 patients), Boswellia serrata (the only one of seven supplements to reach significance for WOMAC pain in a 2025 network meta-analysis), vitamin D3 where deficiency is present.</li>
        <li><strong>Mixed, and weakening:</strong> curcumin — the reviews behind the "as good as ibuprofen" claim were themselves rated of extremely low quality in 2026 — plus oral hyaluronic acid and omega-3.</li>
        <li><strong>Recommended against:</strong> glucosamine, chondroitin and the two combined, per the ACR's September 2026 update, for knee, hip and hand osteoarthritis alike.</li>
        <li><strong>Weak or insufficient evidence:</strong> MSM, Cissus quadrangularis, most proprietary "joint blends".</li>
        <li><strong>More effective than all of the above:</strong> quadriceps strengthening and weight management — both strong recommendations in that same guideline.</li>
      </ul>
