      <h2 id="worth-it">The ones worth your money</h2>

      <h3>1. Collagen peptides — 5,000–10,000mg daily</h3>
      <p>
        The best-supported option, and the one with trials designed around function rather than
        laboratory markers. Clark and colleagues followed 147 athletes for 24 weeks on 10g of
        collagen hydrolysate daily and found <strong>significant reductions in joint pain when
        walking, standing, carrying and lifting</strong>. Zdzieblik and colleagues replicated the
        pattern at 5g daily over 12 weeks in young adults with functional knee pain. If you are loading
        these knees in a gym, see also <a href="/pages/collagen-for-men">collagen for men</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Mechanism: absorbed peptides appear to signal chondrocytes — cartilage cells — to increase
        matrix production, and they supply glycine and proline, the amino acids that dominate
        collagen's structure. Type I collagen also makes up around 90% of the organic matrix of
        bone, so the benefit is not limited to cartilage — see
        <a href="/pages/how-to-increase-bone-density">how to increase bone density</a> for what
        actually moves a DEXA score.
      </p>
      <p>
        The catch: <strong>dose</strong>. Most joint capsules contain a few hundred milligrams.
        Nothing below 2.5g has meaningful supporting evidence, and the joint trials clustered at
        5–10g. Read the full breakdown in
        <a href="/pages/collagen-for-joint-pain">collagen for joint pain</a>, and the safety record
        — including the fish-allergy contraindication that applies to every marine collagen — in
        <a href="/pages/collagen-side-effects">collagen side effects</a>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Two newer syntheses sharpen this picture, and they point in opposite directions, so both
        belong here. An updated meta-analysis in <em>Clinical and Experimental Rheumatology</em>
        (2024) pooled 11 randomised trials in 870 knee osteoarthritis patients and found collagen
        beat placebo on <strong>WOMAC pain (mean difference −13.63, 95% CI −20.67 to −6.58)</strong>
        and on function (−6.46, 95% CI −9.52 to −3.40). Heterogeneity was very high, though — I² of
        88% and 75% respectively — which means the individual trials disagreed with one another a
        great deal.
      </p>

      <p>
        Against that, a 2025 Bayesian network meta-analysis in <em>Nutrients</em> put seven
        supplements in direct competition across 39 trials and 4,599 patients. In its WOMAC pain
        analysis (35 studies, 4,015 patients), <strong>collagen trended better than placebo but did
        not reach statistical significance</strong>, and ranked low among the seven; it placed
        fourth for stiffness. That is an uncomfortable result for a collagen company to print, so
        here is the honest reading of it: collagen has more supporting trials than almost anything
        else on this page, and when it is ranked head to head against other supplements on a single
        pain scale, it does not come out on top.
      </p>

      <h3>2. Vitamin D3 — 600–2,000 IU daily</h3>
      <p>
        Not glamorous, and the one I check first in clinic. Studies of urban Indian populations have
        repeatedly reported <strong>vitamin D deficiency in 70–90% of adults</strong> — a
        remarkable figure in a country with abundant sunlight, driven by indoor work, sun
        avoidance, pollution and skin pigmentation.
      </p>
      <p>
        Vitamin D governs how much dietary calcium you actually absorb. Deficiency causes diffuse
        musculoskeletal pain and proximal muscle weakness in its own right, and weak quadriceps is
        one of the strongest risk factors for knee osteoarthritis progression.
      </p>
      <p>
        Important nuance, stated plainly: supplementing vitamin D in people who are
        <strong>already replete</strong> does not improve osteoarthritis outcomes. This is a
        deficiency correction, not a treatment. Get a 25-hydroxyvitamin D level tested — it is an
        inexpensive test and it converts guesswork into a decision. Which symptoms are genuinely
        specific to deficiency — and which of the ones you will read online are not — is set out in
        <a href="/pages/vitamin-d-deficiency-symptoms">vitamin D deficiency symptoms</a>.
      </p>

      <h3>3. Boswellia serrata — and we do not sell it</h3>
      <p>
        Boswellia has moved further than anything else on this list in the last two years, and it
        is not in <a href="/products/skelecoll">Skelecoll</a>. In the 2025 <em>Nutrients</em>
        network meta-analysis described above, <strong>Boswellia was the only one of the seven
        supplements to reach statistical significance for WOMAC pain</strong> — a mean difference
        of 10.58 against placebo, 95% CI 6.45 to 14.78 — and it ranked first for both pain and
        stiffness. Earlier pooled analyses pointed the same way.
      </p>
      <p>
        The usual caveats apply: the Boswellia trial base is small, several studies are short or
        industry-sponsored, and heterogeneity across the pooled estimates is high. But on the
        comparative evidence as it stands today, if you are buying one standalone ingredient for
        osteoarthritic knee pain, Boswellia has the better claim. I would rather write that than
        pretend our own formula covers everything worth taking.
      </p>

      <h3>4. Curcumin — around 1,000mg daily of a bioavailable form</h3>
      <p>
        Curcumin inhibits NF-κB, a central regulator of the inflammatory cascade. A 2016
        meta-analysis in the <em>Journal of Medicinal Food</em> pooled randomised trials and found
        roughly 1,000mg per day <strong>improved osteoarthritis symptoms</strong>, with several
        head-to-head trials showing pain relief comparable to ibuprofen and a better
        gastrointestinal side-effect profile.
      </p>
      <p>
        The caveat is absorption. Plain turmeric powder is poorly bioavailable — you need a
        formulated version (piperine-enhanced, phospholipid-complexed or water-soluble). "Contains
        turmeric" on a label tells you very little.
      </p>

      <p>
        One update since this article was first published, and it points downward. A 2026 critical
        review examined seven systematic reviews of curcumin for knee osteoarthritis and judged
        them <strong>of extremely low methodological quality</strong> — poor reporting, substantial
        overlap in the trials they each drew on, and evidence graded mostly low across 48 outcomes.
        The 2025 network meta-analysis also failed to find a significant WOMAC pain effect for
        curcumin. The honest position is that curcumin probably does something useful for
        inflammatory knee pain, and that the literature claiming it matches ibuprofen is a good
        deal weaker than its confident tone suggests.
      </p>

      <h2 id="mixed">The mixed cases</h2>

      <h3>Glucosamine sulphate — 1,500mg daily</h3>
      <p>
        The default joint supplement for three decades, and the evidence is no longer genuinely
        contested — it has simply gone against glucosamine. The <strong>GAIT trial</strong>
        (<em>New England Journal of Medicine</em>, 2006) randomised nearly 1,600 knee
        osteoarthritis patients and found glucosamine and chondroitin
        <strong>no better than placebo</strong> overall. A subgroup with moderate-to-severe pain
        benefited from the combination, but that result was exploratory and has not reliably
        replicated.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>This is the part of the article that changed most this year.</strong> In September
        2026 the American College of Rheumatology published an update to its 2019 osteoarthritis
        guideline, and it <strong>strongly recommends against glucosamine, chondroitin and
        combination preparations of the two</strong> — now for hand osteoarthritis as well as knee
        and hip, where that strong recommendation already stood. In guideline language, "strongly
        recommends against" is about as unambiguous as the format allows.
      </p>
      <p>
        Since <a href="/products/skelecoll">Skelecoll</a> contains 300mg of glucosamine HCl, I
        should say plainly what that means rather than leave you to work it out. It is a fifth of
        the 1,500mg used in the trials; it is the hydrochloride salt rather than the sulphate form
        that performed better in the European studies; and the body of evidence underneath it is
        one that rheumatologists now advise against. It sits in the formula as a minor co-factor on
        the lubrication side, alongside 120mg of hyaluronic acid. <strong>It is not a reason to buy
        the product, and I would not defend it as one.</strong>
      </p>

      <h3>Chondroitin sulphate — 800–1,200mg daily</h3>
      <p>
        Same trial history, same verdict, same 2026 guideline recommendation against it, and
        usually sold alongside glucosamine. Some evidence for
        slowing joint space narrowing on imaging; less convincing evidence that patients feel
        better. Quality control is also a genuine problem — independent testing has repeatedly
        found chondroitin products containing substantially less than the labelled amount.
      </p>

      <h3>Oral hyaluronic acid — 80–200mg daily</h3>
      <p>
        Hyaluronic acid is the molecule that makes synovial fluid viscous, and injected HA is an
        established treatment. The oral case is weaker but not absent: several small trials,
        including work by Oe and colleagues, reported improved knee pain scores over 8–12 weeks at
        doses around 200mg. Sensible as part of a stack; thin as a standalone purchase.
      </p>

      <h3>Omega-3 fatty acids — 1,000–3,000mg EPA/DHA</h3>
      <p>
        Strong evidence in <em>rheumatoid</em> arthritis, which is an autoimmune inflammatory
        disease. Much weaker in osteoarthritis, which is primarily mechanical. Worth taking for
        cardiovascular and general health; do not expect it to resolve a worn knee.
      </p>

      <h2 id="weak">The oversold ones</h2>

      <h3>MSM (methylsulfonylmethane)</h3>
      <p>
        Appears in almost every joint blend. The trials are small, short and mostly of low quality,
        with inconsistent results. It is not harmful and it is cheap, but it is present in most
        formulas because it is inexpensive filler with a scientific-sounding name, not because the
        data demands it.
      </p>

      <h3>Cissus quadrangularis</h3>
      <p>
        A traditional Ayurvedic preparation with a long history in fracture healing and some
        preliminary work on bone and tendon. I will be straightforward, including about our own
        formula: <strong>the modern clinical evidence base for knee osteoarthritis is thin.</strong>
        <a href="/products/skelecoll">Skelecoll</a> includes it for muscular endurance around the
        joint on the strength of traditional use and early data — not because it has been proven in
        the way collagen peptides have.
      </p>

      <h3>Proprietary "joint complex" blends</h3>
      <p>
        If a label lists a "proprietary blend" with a single total weight and no per-ingredient
        doses, assume the expensive actives are present in trace amounts and the cheap ones make up
        the mass. This is the most common way an underdosed product is made to look comprehensive.
        <strong>If you cannot see a milligram figure next to each active, do not buy it.</strong>
      </p>

