      </p>

      <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>

      <div class="jr-faq">
        <details>
          <summary>What is the best supplement for knee pain?</summary>
          <p>
            Collagen peptides at 5–10g daily have the largest randomised trial base for
            activity-related knee pain, and Boswellia serrata has the strongest recent comparative
            result — it was the only one of seven supplements to reach statistical significance
            for WOMAC pain in a 2025 network meta-analysis. Vitamin D3 matters where deficiency is
            present. No supplement outperforms quadriceps strengthening and weight management.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does glucosamine actually work for knees?</summary>
          <p>
            The American College of Rheumatology's September 2026 guideline update strongly
            recommends against glucosamine, chondroitin and combination preparations of the two,
            for knee, hip and hand osteoarthritis alike. That follows the large independent GAIT
            trial, which found them no better than placebo overall. Glucosamine is cheap and safe,
            so taking it is not harmful, but it should not be where your money goes first.
            Skelecoll contains 300mg of glucosamine HCl as a minor co-factor, which is a fifth of
            the 1,500mg trial dose.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How long do knee pain supplements take to work?</summary>
          <p>
            Allow 12 weeks. Collagen trials measured significant pain reduction between weeks 8
            and 24, and curcumin trials typically ran 6 to 12 weeks. Anything promising relief in
            days is describing an anti-inflammatory effect, not tissue change.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Should I take vitamin D for knee pain?</summary>
          <p>
            Only if you are deficient — but in urban India that is likely, with studies reporting
            deficiency in 70–90% of adults. Vitamin D deficiency itself causes musculoskeletal
            pain and muscle weakness. Supplementing when already replete does not improve
            osteoarthritis outcomes, so get a 25-hydroxyvitamin D level tested first.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is turmeric as good as ibuprofen for joint pain?</summary>
          <p>
            Several head-to-head trials found bioavailable curcumin at around 1,000mg daily
            produced pain relief comparable to ibuprofen in knee osteoarthritis, with fewer
            gastrointestinal side effects. That claim is weaker than it sounds: a 2026 critical
            review judged seven systematic reviews of curcumin for knee osteoarthritis to be of
            extremely low methodological quality. Plain turmeric powder is also poorly absorbed,
            so the formulation matters as much as the dose.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can I take collagen and glucosamine together?</summary>
          <p>
            Yes — there is no known interaction and they act on different parts of the joint.
            Collagen peptides support cartilage matrix production, while glucosamine and
            hyaluronic acid support the proteoglycan and lubrication side. Skelecoll combines both
            in a single daily shot.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Which knee supplement has the strongest evidence right now?</summary>
          <p>
            It depends what you mean by strongest. Collagen peptides have the largest trial base:
            an updated 2024 meta-analysis of 11 randomised trials in 870 patients found
            significant improvements in WOMAC pain and function, though with high heterogeneity.
            Boswellia serrata has the best comparative result: in a 2025 network meta-analysis of
            seven supplements across 39 trials, it was the only one to reach statistical
            significance for WOMAC pain. Neither outperforms exercise.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Do weight-loss injections help knee osteoarthritis?</summary>
          <p>
            The September 2026 ACR guideline conditionally recommends a GLP-1 receptor agonist,
            alongside diet and exercise, for people who have knee osteoarthritis together with
            obesity. The reasoning is mechanical: loads across the knee are amplified several
            times over during walking and stair climbing, so weight loss reduces joint load
            directly. This is a prescription decision for your doctor, not a supplement, and it
            does not replace strengthening work.
          </p>
